# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cad-sweep-fails-jobs-for-zombie-pids — the sweep failed 21 CAD jobs over acad.exe processes that had already exited
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: ci
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-sedit-path-and-alfe-list-hosts
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

Master pipeline 2885566068, native child 2885566207 (2026-09-26): *21 of its
CAD jobs failed*, every one of them in =scripts/sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1= before
running anything of its own — =pathname:probe:*=, =optkw:probe:*=,
=getcname:probe:*=, =path-sysvars:probe:*=, =pgp:probe:*=,
=encoding:experiment:*=, =verify:bricscad-hidden-ui:windows=, … The trace of
each (e.g. job 16755546845):

#+begin_example
cad sweep: 3 instance(s) recorded (3 line(s) in C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-created-cad.txt)
cad sweep: ending orphaned acad.exe pid 21928 (created 20260925132249.056071+120)
cad sweep: pid 21928 survived Stop-Process; taskkill /T /F
cad sweep:   Erreur : le processus de PID 21928 (processus enfant de PID 1760)
             n'a pas été arrêté.
cad sweep:   Raison : Il n'existe aucune instance en cours de cette tâche.
cad sweep: pid 21928 SURVIVED taskkill too
   … identically for pid 17640 (of the previous day) and pid 9164 …
cad sweep: 0 ended, 3 left
cad sweep: FAILING THIS JOB -- a CAD process could not be ended.
#+end_example

* Cause

Read taskkill's reason: /Il n'existe aucune instance en cours de cette tâche/ —
"there is no running instance of the task". **The processes were already
dead.** Windows keeps a process-table entry until the last HANDLE to it is
closed, and =acad.exe='s parent here is the COM service (pid 1760), which holds
one — so a dead =acad.exe= lingers as a zombie: no threads left,
=Stop-Process= cannot touch it, =taskkill= refuses it, and =Get-Process -Id=
keeps answering for days.

The sweep asked only whether the id still answered =Get-Process=, took "yes"
for "still running", and failed the job. So the gate fired on processes that
blocked nothing: a dead process answers no COM call, holds no ActiveX
registration, and cannot be the reason a CAD session fails.

Two entries dated from 2026-09-25, so every CAD job on the runner had been
failing at the gate for a day — the gate that exists to protect those jobs was
the only thing stopping them. Worse than the original problem it fixed:
=autocad-orphaned-by-killed-or-cancelled-jobs= at least let a job run.

* Fixed in code — clautolisp 2.2.110

=Get-CadProcessState= classifies a recorded id explicitly before anything is
killed:

- =gone= — no such process; entry dropped.
- =zombie= — the id exists but the process has exited (=ThreadCount= 0, or
  =HasExited=): nothing to kill and nothing blocked, so it is reported as
  settled and the entry is dropped. Previously this was the "survivor".
- =live= — really running: =Stop-Process=, then =taskkill /T /F=, then
  re-classified. Only an instance still =live= after that lands in the
  survivors list, and only that list fails the job.

The failure path, its message and the =CAD_SWEEP_IGNORE_SURVIVORS=1= escape are
unchanged for the case they were written for — a genuinely wedged AutoCAD
holding the COM registration.

Regression tests (=autolisp-front-end/tests/backend-cad-tests.lisp=):
=cad-sweep-classifies-a-process-before-calling-it-a-survivor= and
=cad-sweep-fails-only-for-a-cad-that-is-really-running=. There is no PowerShell
on the Linux lanes, so they assert on the script TEXT — the instrument the
VBScript-template tests already use, for the same reason: what breaks is a
property of the file another tool reads. Verified RED against the pre-fix
script (no classifier at all; two bare =Get-Process= presence checks).

Documented in =alfe-user-manual.org= § /When alfe is killed before it can quit
AutoCAD/ ("a process id that still answers is not a running process").

* Open criterion — ANSWERED on the runner, 2026-09-27 (job 16761201255)

=verify:autocad-activex:windows= ran on PF5S26B at 09:12:46Z and its sweep
printed, verbatim:

#+begin_example
cad sweep: 5 instance(s) recorded (8 line(s) in C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-created-cad.txt)
cad sweep: pid 21928 has EXITED (no threads left) and is waiting to be reaped; nothing to kill, entry dropped
cad sweep: pid 17640 has EXITED (no threads left) and is waiting to be reaped; nothing to kill, entry dropped
cad sweep: pid  9164 has EXITED (no threads left) and is waiting to be reaped; nothing to kill, entry dropped
cad sweep: pid 32840 is gone; entry dropped
cad sweep: pid 35184 is gone; entry dropped
cad sweep: 0 ended, 5 already settled, 0 still running
#+end_example

Every part of the criterion, and nothing inferred:

- *pids 21928 and 17640 are the two from the original incident*, recorded in
  this ticket in August. They are reported as EXITED-and-unreaped — zombies —
  not as survivors. The earlier reading of them as "wedged instances holding
  the COM registration" is now settled as wrong, from the machine rather than
  from reasoning.
- *The three states are distinguished*: exited-but-unreaped, gone, and running.
  The distinction is the whole fix.
- *The registry is pruned*: 8 lines collapsed to 5 instances and every entry
  dropped. (The same runner's earlier job at 09:01Z reported "nothing recorded"
  for that file, which is what the empty state looks like.)
- *The job was NOT failed.* It went on to run =verify-autocad-activex.ps1=,
  which is the behaviour the pre-2.2.110 sweep denied to 22 jobs.

So the fix is now observed on the platform, not only reasoned from a trace and
unit tests. Nothing named in this ticket remains. Moved to =issues/closed/=.

*What the same job found next is a DIFFERENT defect* and belongs to
=alfe-autocad-hung-instance-blocks-com-bootstrap=: with the sweep reporting no
live instance, the COM bridge still failed — =ATTACHED=0 CREATED=1= and a
rejection on the document calls. That is our own freshly created AutoCAD being
busy, not an orphan, and it is fixed there.

* Note

Filed per AGENTS.md's rule for a bug found while doing something else. The
sweep script is this project's own (added for
=autocad-orphaned-by-killed-or-cancelled-jobs=), so this is a self-inflicted
defect, which is exactly why it gets a ticket rather than a silent amendment.
